@meta{
  id: cli.tui
}

`sisho tui [PATH]`: a workbench that runs in the terminal. Look around the workspace, migrate Markdown, fix metadata in batches, and search and replace by structure.

Status: implemented. It was `tomet tui` and moved here, with its structure unchanged; the code is `crates/sisho-tui`.

The options are in `sisho tui --help`. This document says what the four tabs do and what is written when.

=[ Starting ]

- `PATH` is the directory or file to open. Without it, `.`.
- `-c, --config <FILE>` points at a config file (`.tmt`). Without it, the config found by walking up from `PATH` is used, and without one, the default. The config decides the output of a migration (how a document converted from Markdown is written) and how structural search picks its targets (`workspace.ignore`).
- On start it reads the whole workspace and parses it. While it does, it shows "Scanning workspace..." and `Ctrl+C` or `Esc` quits.
- The terminal is given back as it was however the TUI ends: a normal quit, an error while the workspace is being read, or a panic. (`tomet tui` gave it back only at the end of a normal run.)
- With no terminal to take over (standard input is not one), it says so and exits 1 without drawing anything.

=[ The screen ]

Four tabs on top, a list on the left and a preview on the right in the middle, a line of actions and a line of status below. The mouse works (clicking tabs and rows, scrolling the preview).

@table
|[ Tab ][ Name           ][ The list shows                  ][ The right side shows ]
|[ 1   ][ Explorer       ][ the workspace's tree of files   ][ the file's contents ]
|[ 2   ][ Migration      ][ the tree of Markdown to migrate ][ the Markdown beside what it converts to ]
|[ 3   ][ BatchMeta      ][ the `.tmt` documents            ][ the `@meta` fields and the diff of the source once rewritten ]
|[ 4   ][ StructuralGrep ][ the documents the search hit    ][ the diff of the replacement ]

=[ Explorer ]

- Walk the tree. Directories open and close. `/` narrows the tree by a partial match on name and path.
- `e` edits the file in place. `Ctrl+S` saves. Closing without saving asks whether to throw the changes away.
- `E` opens an external editor: the environment variable `EDITOR` (or `VISUAL`, or else `nano`). When it is closed, the workspace is read again. The editor is started through `sisho-run`, like every other process `sisho` starts.

Explorer is the only tab that writes without asking. It writes only when saved with `Ctrl+S`.

=[ Migration ]

Converts Markdown (`.md`) to `.tmt`.

- The list is the tree containing the Markdown that can be migrated. Only the files selected (`Space`, or `a` for all) are the target.
- The preview is the original Markdown beside the result converted according to the config. The converted document gets the `id` the config asks for.
- Running it asks for confirmation, then writes each converted document next to its source, with the extension changed to `.tmt`. The original Markdown is not deleted. A file already converted is marked and is not converted again.

=[ BatchMeta ]

Rewrites one field of `@meta` in the documents selected, all at once.

- `k` enters the field name and `v` the value. The field name starts as `author`.
- Running it, with a field given and documents selected, asks `Update meta field '<name>' in <n> file(s)?` and then saves. With an empty name or no document selected, it says so and does nothing.

=[ StructuralGrep ]

Searches and replaces by syntax, not by string. The target is every `.tmt`.

- There are three conditions: the name of an element (`t`), a key such as `@meta`'s (`k`), and a string contained in the value (`v`). `s` searches and lists the documents hit. `c` clears the conditions.
- Enter what to replace with (`r`) and run it. What happens depends on the conditions.
@table
|[ Conditions                 ][ Replacement ]
|[ key, value and replacement ][ the value of that key is replaced with the replacement ]
|[ key and replacement        ][ the key is renamed to the replacement ]
|[ name and replacement       ][ the element is renamed to the replacement ]
- Running it shows the number of documents selected, asks for confirmation, and then saves. With no replacement, it says so and does nothing.

This tab uses `tomet-transform`. `tandr`, when it exists, is meant to take over what it does.

=[ Confirmation and writing ]

What rewrites documents: running Migration, BatchMeta and StructuralGrep, and saving in Explorer.

- Migration, BatchMeta and StructuralGrep always ask before they run. `y` or `Enter` goes on; `n` or `Esc` stops.
- The diff before and after is already in the preview before the question.
- After writing, the list and the preview are read again.

=[ Keys ]

@table
|[ Key                         ][ What it does ]
|[ `Tab`, `Shift+Tab`, `1`-`4` ][ switch tabs ]
|[ `Up` `Down`, `k` `j`        ][ move in the list; scroll when in the preview ]
|[ `Left` `Right`              ][ open and close directories; move between panes (list, preview, actions) ]
|[ `Space`, `a`                ][ in Explorer `Space` opens and closes a directory; in other tabs it selects (`Space`) or selects all (`a`) ]
|[ `Enter`                     ][ in Explorer opens and closes a directory; in Migration selects; in BatchMeta and StructuralGrep runs ]
|[ `e`, `E`                    ][ in Explorer edits (`e` in place, `E` in an external editor); in other tabs runs. These two are all that runs Migration ]
|[ `PageUp`, `PageDown`        ][ scroll the preview ]
|[ `q`, `Esc`, `Ctrl+C`        ][ `q` and `Ctrl+C` always quit; `Esc` returns to the list, and from the list quits ]

=[ Not decided ]

- An option to delete the original Markdown after a migration. The engine (`MigrationEngine`) accepts it, but the screen does not offer it.
- Fixing several fields at once in BatchMeta.
